    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. The \"army in a closet\" thing is a good way to put it. \n\nCan you dive a bit more into the risks of vertical scaling? Specifically, what happens when we hit that \"limit\" you mentioned, and what are the main downsides to just sticking with a single large instance instead of moving to horizontal?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The primary risk of vertical scaling is hitting a hard ceiling, which is a very real, often very expensive, bottleneck. When you max out the hardware of a single machine, you've essentially reached the absolute limit of what that one chassis can handle. The main downside is that you've created a single point of failure; if that one massive server goes down, your entire operation stops. This is much riskier than if you had ten smaller servers, where one failure only means a small portion of the service is affected.",
      "source": "target",
