    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. one more thing for the brief: how do these two approaches handle sudden, unpredictable spikes in traffic? like if we get a massive surge in users all at once, which one reacts better and why?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For sudden, unpredictable traffic spikes, horizontal scaling is vastly superior. It allows for rapid elasticity, meaning you can quickly spin up more instances—like deploying a whole new squad of workers instantly—to absorb the surge. With vertical scaling, you are stuck waiting for hardware upgrades, which takes time, and you risk crashing the single, overloaded machine before the upgrade even arrives. Think of it this way: horizontal scaling is like having a swarm of tiny, adaptable workers, while vertical scaling is like having one gigantic, slow-to-reconfigure behemoth.",
      "source": "target",
